14	{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:23:00Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"upskill_reflect","detail":"Considered writing a new playbook 'Filter long-lived streaming-RPC spans before believing an error-span rate' — flagd EventStream renewals were the entire error signal this shift. Searched existing playbooks/experiences: no coverage. Ran blind challenge via subagent.","reasoning":"Challenge surfaced two failures. (1) The 'DEADLINE_EXCEEDED on close is normal for server-streaming RPCs' claim is flagd-SDK-specific, not a gRPC convention — codifying it as a rule generalizes lore. (2) A genuinely broken stream (server crash-loop, upstream timeout, path degradation closing streams early) can present exactly the same shape (same name, code 4, roughly periodic); the rule as written would tell me to filter and re-evaluate — but if the failure IS the stream, the residual is zero and I would silently declare nothing wrong. That is precisely 'blinding myself,' which the personality forbids. The revised form the reviewer suggested collapses back into the existing 'triangulate before you believe it' heuristic. Writing something unneeded is worse than writing nothing — no new file."}
